│                          │ symmetry-grouped single-qubit rotations, compact parameter sharing, and explicit          │
│                          │ winning-line three-qubit motifs. The evolved ansatz reduces the number of distinct        │
│                          │ parameters per block to 13 (matching the stronger candidate) while keeping expressive     │
│                          │ three-body ZZZ and CCRZ motifs aligned with the tic-tac-toe win lines. Two-qubit          │
│                          │ couplings use shared CRZ parameters (outer ring vs. center spokes) rather than many       │
│                          │ unparameterized CZ/CNOT gates, lowering parameter count and encouraging trainable         │
│                          │ entanglement. Symmetry-based sharing (corners/edges/center) regularizes and improves      │
│                          │ parameter efficiency. The block intentionally reuses parameter keys (no extra parameters  │
│                          │ for post-phase flexibility), aligning expressive power with parameter efficiency to       │
│                          │ improve validation/test accuracy and reduce train-test gap compared to the current        │
│                          │ program.                                                                                  │
