│ patch_name               │ split_triples_and_ring_entanglement                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the current monolithic ZZZ/CCRZ motif and shared CRZ grid with a geometry-aware   │
│                          │ ansatz:                                                                                   │
│                          │ - Keep symmetry-grouped local RY rotations.                                               │
│                          │ - Replace the single shared ZZZ and CCRZ line parameters with separated ZZZ parameters    │
│                          │ for rows/columns/diagonals and separated CCRZ parameters for edge-center vs diag-center   │
│                          │ triples. This lets the circuit represent different three-body correlations on winning     │
│                          │ lines more flexibly.                                                                      │
│                          │ - Replace the single CRZ grid parameter with a ring-style CRZ ("crz_ring") and a separate │
│                          │ center-edge CRZ ("crz_center_edge") to allow the center coupling to differ from           │
│                          │ outer-ring couplings.                                                                     │
│                          │ - Retain shared post-RZ rotations by symmetry group for phase flexibility /               │
│                          │ regularization.                                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This change increases the per-block parameterization (more targeted triple params and     │
│                          │ split two-qubit params), which earlier experiments showed improves validation/test        │
│                          │ accuracy and reduces generalization gap for this task by better matching the problem's    │
│                          │ geometric motifs (winning lines and center-role).                                         │
