╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ diversify_three_qubit_motifs_with_ccrx                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The current program achieves a combined score of 0.68 with 84 parameters and relatively   │
│                          │ high validation loss (1.99). The prior programs achieved 0.72 with 78 parameters and      │
│                          │ better validation loss (1.89).                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The recommendation suggests diversifying three-qubit motifs by alternating CCRZ and       │
│                          │ controlled-RX across repeats. This approach:                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Preserves parameter efficiency**: By keeping the same total parameter count but      │
│                          │ swapping gate types between repeats, we maintain the two-in-a-row logic while broadening  │
│                          │ the expressiveness of line correlations.                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Improves generalization**: The current program has a high generalization gap (0.05)  │
│                          │ and validation loss (1.99). Alternating gate types can help the circuit learn more robust │
│                          │ features by exploring different correlation structures.                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Reduces overfitting**: The prior best program (0.72 score) had lower validation loss │
│                          │ and better generalization. By introducing CCRX gates (controlled-RX instead of            │
│                          │ controlled-RZ), we add rotational diversity that may help the optimizer find better local │
│                          │ minima.                                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 4. **Maintains connectivity**: Both CCRZ and CCRX are three-qubit gates with no           │
│                          │ connectivity restrictions, so they can operate on the same winning-line triples.          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The strategy is to:                                                                       │
│                          │ - Keep CCRZ gates on all eight winning lines in repeat 0 (as currently implemented)       │
│                          │ - Replace them with CRX gates (controlled-RX) in repeat 1, using new parameter names      │
│                          │ `crx_horiz`, `crx_vert`, `crx_diag` to match the geometric categories                     │
│                          │ - This doubles the expressiveness of line-based correlations while keeping the same       │
│                          │ parameter structure                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This aligns with the motif-discovery philosophy: the circuit can now express both Z-basis │
│                          │ and X-basis controlled rotations on winning lines, giving the optimizer more flexibility  │
│                          │ to learn the tic-tac-toe classification task.                                             │
