                        Patch Metadata - Gen 64/100 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_lattice_classifier                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ This proposal uses a fundamentally different algorithmic approach: instead of a fixed     │
│                          │ symmetric parameter-sharing scheme, we use an **adaptive lattice representation** that    │
│                          │ treats the tic-tac-toe grid as a learnable factor graph. The key innovations are:         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Sparse, targeted entanglement**: Rather than applying all ZZZ and CCRZ gates to      │
│                          │ every winning line (which over-parameterizes and causes high gate count/depth), we apply  │
│                          │ a minimal set of strategically positioned two-qubit gates with learned multiplexing       │
│                          │ coefficients.                                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Efficient basis rotation hierarchy**: We replace the 6-layer symmetry-grouped        │
│                          │ approach with a 3-layer compact design:                                                   │
│                          │    - **Layer 1**: Fast local rotations (RY only on corners/edges/center with 3 shared     │
│                          │ params)                                                                                   │
│                          │    - **Layer 2**: Learned grid connectivity via CRZ on ring + selective center couplings  │
│                          │    - **Layer 3**: Single post-rotation layer (RZ only)                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Reduced parameter count**: By eliminating duplicate symmetry groups and removing     │
│                          │ redundant RX/RZ post-layers, we drop from 102 params to ~40–50 params per block,          │
│                          │ improving generalization and reducing the train-validation gap.                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 4. **Measurement fusion**: The output expectation values use a simplified linear          │
│                          │ combination that learns class boundaries more efficiently, reducing test loss.            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The algorithm maintains the same quantum circuit structure (3 uploads × 2 repeats) and    │
│                          │ measurement framework but replaces parameter-heavy baroque layers with a minimal,         │
│                          │ learnable grid-graph representation. This should achieve higher test accuracy with lower  │
│                          │ generalization gap through better regularization via parameter efficiency.                │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0792                                                                                   │
