                        Patch Metadata - Gen 66/100 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ crx-crz-perimeter-spoke                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ Key parameters in the original approach:                                                  │
│                          │ - State preparation: symmetry-grouped RY rotations (corner/edge/center).                  │
│                          │ - Entangling: a single shared CRZ for the ring and a shared CRZ for spokes.               │
│                          │ - Higher-order motifs: shared ZZZ (rows/cols/diags) and CCRZ for center motifs.           │
│                          │ - Output flexibility: symmetry-grouped RZ post-rotations.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Why this change:                                                                          │
│                          │ - I keep the symmetry-inspired parameter sharing for single-qubit rotations and           │
│                          │ post-phases (efficient, low-variance), but I add a light, complementary XY-style          │
│                          │ perimeter coupling (CRX) across alternating ring edges. This introduces a different       │
│                          │ interaction axis (X) in addition to CRZ's phase coupling so the circuit can represent     │
│                          │ correlations that mix X and Z correlations more effectively.                              │
│                          │ - I refine the CRZ couplings into two semantic groups that the optimizer can tune         │
│                          │ independently: crz_perimeter (outer ring) and crz_spoke (edges-to-center). This follows   │
│                          │ the suggested perimeter/spoke pattern while keeping the parameter count modest.           │
│                          │ - The ZZZ and CCRZ motif layers are preserved (geometric grouping) so the ansatz retains  │
│                          │ strong inductive bias toward the 8 winning lines, but the added CRX provides richer local │
│                          │ entangling structure to help the optimizer map the classical features into those motifs.  │
│                          │ - Parameter efficiency: only one extra shared parameter (crx_perimeter) is added compared │
│                          │ to the current program. That increases expressivity without a large parameter-budget      │
│                          │ penalty.                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Training / optimization settings (kept unchanged by the fixed training loop):             │
│                          │ - Adam optimizer with the same default learning rate handling; same epochs, batch size,   │
│                          │ early stopping, and data splits.                                                          │
│                          │ - The changes are intended to: (1) improve representational expressivity by combining Z-  │
│                          │ and X-type controlled couplings, (2) keep symmetry-sharing to reduce variance and         │
│                          │ encourage generalization, and (3) keep a small net increase in parameter count to avoid   │
│                          │ overfitting while improving validation/test performance.                                  │
