│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ fixed-cz-winning-line-ccrz                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the original CRZ-heavy entangling layer with fixed CZ entanglement across the     │
│                          │ grid edges and concentrate the trainable multi-qubit motif on the actual winning lines.   │
│                          │ Rationale:                                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ - Fixed CZs provide stronger, parameter-free entangling connectivity across the board so  │
│                          │ feature information can propagate without increasing parameter count; this often improves │
│                          │ generalization by reducing overfitting to entangling-phase parameters.                    │
│                          │ - Replacing the ring/shared CRZs with fixed CZs frees up capacity in the parameter        │
│                          │ budget. I reallocate that capacity by placing a single shared CCRZ parameter across all   │
│                          │ eight winning lines, letting the ansatz directly condition multi-qubit rotations on the   │
│                          │ canonical tic-tac-toe lines (controls on the outer cells, target the middle cell of each  │
│                          │ line). This focuses trainable nonlocal interactions on the geometry relevant to the task. │
│                          │ - Keep the lightweight symmetry-grouped single-qubit rotations upfront and the            │
│                          │ symmetry-grouped post-RZ rotations for phase flexibility.                                 │
│                          │ - Keep ZZZ motifs grouped by geometric category (rows/cols/diagonals) so collective Z     │
│                          │ rotations still capture parity-like features of lines.                                    │
│                          │ - Overall this lowers the per-block parameter count (reducing overfitting risk) while     │
│                          │ increasing entangling expressivity targeted at winning lines, which should improve test   │
│                          │ accuracy and reduce train-test gap.                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ All gates remain within the allowed vocabulary and two-qubit gates only use adjacent grid │
│                          │ edges.                                                                                    │
