│ patch_name               │ perimeter_spoke_motif_ansatz                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ This candidate refines the ansatz to improve generalization and sample efficiency by:     │
│                          │ - Aligning multi-qubit motifs to tic-tac-toe winning geometries: shared ZZZ rotations for │
│                          │ orthogonal rows/columns and shared doubly-controlled RZ (CCRZ) for the two diagonals.     │
│                          │ This encourages the circuit to directly capture triple-wise correlations relevant to the  │
│                          │ task.                                                                                     │
│                          │ - Splitting the trainable CRZ lattice into two semantic parameters: crz_perimeter (the 8  │
│                          │ outer rim edges) and crz_spoke (the 4 center spokes). This gives the optimizer            │
│                          │ independent control over rim vs hub couplings while keeping gate count constant.          │
│                          │ - Enforcing symmetry-aware parameter sharing (corner / edge / center) for local rotations │
│                          │ (pre/post RZ and an RY mixing layer). This reduces the number of unique parameters per    │
│                          │ block, regularizes the model, and reduces overfitting risk.                               │
│                          │ - Keeping the ansatz compact (13 parameters per block, matching previous seeds) so the    │
│                          │ repeated re-upload/p> structure remains efficient while providing motif-aligned           │
│                          │ expressivity.                                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The result is a parameter-efficient, motif-targeted block expected to reduce train-test   │
│                          │ gap, lower L2 validation loss, and improve test accuracy in fewer Adam steps by focusing  │
│                          │ learning capacity on geometrically relevant interactions.                                 │
